Now the region preset fixes — `regionRatio`, `saveRegionRatio`, `regionLabel`, `cycleRegionPreset`, `setPreset`. Current code:

```kotlin
    private fun regionRatio(): Rect {
        val l = prefs.getFloat("reg_l", 0.025f)
        val t = prefs.getFloat("reg_t", 0.55f)
        val r = prefs.getFloat("reg_r", 0.975f)
        val b = prefs.getFloat("reg_b", 0.98f)
        val s = screenSize()
        return Rect(...)
    }

    private fun saveRegionRatio(rectPx: Rect) { ... putString("reg_preset", "custom") ... }

    private fun regionLabel(): String = when (prefs.getString("reg_preset", "bottom")) {...}

    private fun cycleRegionPreset() {...}

    private fun setPreset(name: String, rectPx: Rect) {...}
```

New versions using Region:

```kotlin
    private fun regionRatio(): Rect {
        val l = prefs.getFloat("reg_l", Region.DEFAULT_RATIOS[0])
        val t = prefs.getFloat("reg_t", Region.DEFAULT_RATIOS[1])
        val r = prefs.getFloat("reg_r", Region.DEFAULT_RATIOS[2])
        val b = prefs.getFloat("reg_b", Region.DEFAULT_RATIOS[3])
        val s = screenSize()
        val box = Region.boxFromRatios(l, t, r, b, s.x, s.y)
        return Rect(box.left, box.top, box.right, box.bottom)
    }

    private fun saveRegionRatio(rectPx: Rect, preset: String? = null) {
        val s = screenSize()
        val box = Region.Box(rectPx.left, rectPx.top, rectPx.right, rectPx.bottom)
        val r = Region.ratiosOf(box, s.x, s.y)
        prefs.edit()
            .putFloat("reg_l", r[0])
            .putFloat("reg_t", r[1])
            .putFloat("reg_r", r[2])
            .putFloat("reg_b", r[3])
            .putString("reg_preset", preset ?: Region.PRESET_CUSTOM)
            .apply()
        refreshFrame()
        rebuildPanel()
    }

    private fun regionPreset(): String =
        Region.normalize?...
```

Hmm, Region doesn't have `normalize`. Prefs may store arbitrary string; `Region.label` has else → "Своя рамка", and `Region.rectFor` has else → bottom. So passing preset straight through works: unknown → bottom rect and "Своя рамка" label... wait — that's inconsistent: unknown preset → label "Своя рамка" but rectFor → bottom defaults. But prefs only ever contain bottom/full/top/center/custom (written by us). For "custom" → rectFor gives bottom defaults, but custom ratios are read from prefs floats (regionRatio uses the stored ratios, not rectFor) — rectFor("custom") is only used by cycleRegionPreset via nextPreset which never returns "custom". OK. Add `Region.presetOf(prefsKey)`? Simpler: keep direct prefs read with default Region.PRESET_BOTTOM.

Actually to be robust, add to Region:
```kotlin
/** Приводит произвольную строку из префов к валидному пресету (custom остаётся custom). */
fun normalizePreset(preset: String?): String = when (preset) { in CYCLE + PRESET_CUSTOM -> preset; else -> PRESET_BOTTOM }
```
Hmm, `preset in ...` — `CYCLE + PRESET_CUSTOM` creates a list; fine. Or simply:
```kotlin
fun normalizePreset(preset: String?) = if (preset == null) PRESET_BOTTOM else if (CYCLE.contains(preset) || preset == PRESET_CUSTOM) preset else PRESET_BOTTOM
```

Then:
```kotlin
    private fun regionPreset(): String = Region.normalizePreset(prefs.getString("reg_preset", Region.PRESET_BOTTOM))

    private fun regionLabel(): String = Region.label(regionPreset())

    private fun cycleRegionPreset() {
        val s = screenSize()
        val next = Region.nextPreset(regionPreset())
        setPreset(next, Region.rectFor(next, s.x, s.y))
    }

    private fun setPreset(name: String, rectPx: Region.Box) {
        saveRegionRatio(Rect(rectPx.left, rectPx.top, rectPx.right, rectPx.bottom), name)
        frameView?.invalidate()
    }
```

And RegionSelectView "Сброс" button: `sel.setRect(Rect(...))` → use Region.Box. And openRegionSelect passes `sel.currentRect()` → Rect conversion. Let me now rewrite RegionSelectView to use Region.Box internally with Region.clamp for drag. That fixes the inversion bug and makes it testable.

Current RegionSelectView:

```kotlin
    private inner class RegionSelectView : View(this@OverlayService) {
        private var rect = Rect(regionRatio())
        ...
        fun currentRect() = Rect(rect)
        fun setRect(r: Rect) { rect = Rect(r); invalidate() }
        override fun onDraw(c: Canvas) {...uses rect...}
        override fun onTouchEvent(ev) {...}
        private fun zoneFor(x, y) {...}
        private fun applyDrag(s: Point, dx, dy) {...}
    }
```

New version with Box:

```kotlin
    private inner class RegionSelectView : View(this@OverlayService) {
        private var box: Region.Box = regionRatio().let { Region.Box(it.left, it.top, it.right, it.bottom) }
        private val zone = dp(48)
        private val minSide = dp(80)
        ...
        fun currentRect(): Rect = Rect(box.left, box.top, box.right, box.bottom)
        fun setRect(r: Region.Box) { box = r; invalidate() }
        override fun onDraw(c: Canvas) {
            val r = currentRect()
            ... (same drawing code using r)
        }
        override fun onTouchEvent: mode = zoneFor; applyDrag: box = Region.clamp(box, s.x, s.y, minSide, minSide) — but clamp doesn't implement the actual delta semantics!
```

Careful: `Region.clamp` only *clamps* a box; the drag must first apply the delta to the appropriate edge, then clamp. Apply delta:

```kotlin
        private fun applyDrag(s: Point, dx: Int, dy: Int) {
            var l = box.left; var t = box.top; var r = box.right; var b = box.bottom
            when (mode) {
                -6 -> { l += dx; r += dx; t += dy; b += dy }
                1 -> { l += dx; t += dy }
                2 -> { r += dx; t += dy }
                3 -> { l += dx; b += dy }
                4 -> { r += dx; b += dy }
                5 -> l += dx
                6 -> r += dx
                7 -> t += dy
                8 -> b += dy
            }
            box = Region.clamp(Region.Box(l, t, r, b), s.x, s.y, minSide, minSide)
        }
```

Wait — for mode 5 (left side): dragging left side rightwards past `right - minSide` — Region.clamp handles: `left.coerceIn(0, ww - minWidth)` ensures left <= ww - minWidth, but not left <= right - minWidth. Consider box = (l=100, r=500), minSide=80, drag left to l=450 → box(450, 500) → clamp: left=coerceIn(450, 0, ww-80) → 450; right=coerceIn(500, 450+80=530, ww) → 530. Result width 80 ≥ min. So clamp *expands* right to keep min width — no inversion, but right jumps. That's acceptable UX (never inverts). If min > screen size, clamp handles via coerceIn ranges. OK.

